    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by exploring the existing codebase to understand the structure and conventions, particularly focusing on the batch approval implementation that I'll use as a reference for the bulk-reject feature.\nNow let me explore the internal/candidate directory structure:\nNow let me read the existing implementation:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity structure:\nNow I have a full understanding of the codebase. Let me implement the bulk-reject functionality. The key requirements are:\n\n1. Add a new `bulk-reject` handler for POST `/purchases/bulk-reject`\n2. Follow the same patterns as `bulk-approve`:\n   - Authenticate approver\n   - Validate idempotency key\n   - Check that all targets are eligible (submitted, not created by approver, same tenant)\n   - Update status from 'submitted' to 'rejected'\n   - Record audit and action request in one transaction\n   - Support idempotent replays\n3. Use operation name `bulk-reject.v4`\n\nLet me create the implementation:\nNow I'll add the reject handler before the return statement:\nNow I need to update the New function to register the reject route:\nNow let me verify the complete file and test it:\nGood! Now let me build and test the code:\nExcellent! The build succeeded. Now let's run the tests:\nPerfect! The build succeeds. Let me verify the implementation meets all the requirements by reviewing the code one more time:\nLet me verify by checking the complete flow one more time in the code I wrote:\nPerfect! The implementation includes all the key elements:\n\n1. **`fingerprintReject`** - Separate fingerprint function for the reject operation\n2. **`New`** - Updated to register both routes: `/purchases/bulk-approve` and `/purchases/bulk-reject`\n3. **`rejectHandler`** - The new handler implementing bulk rejection\n\nLet me verify the implementation meets all the requirements from the task:\n\n✅ **Path registration**: Routes are registered via `protocol.Routes` in the `New` function\n✅ **HTTP adapter**: Uses shared `protocol.HTTP` adapter that validates input, binds identity, and handles idempotency-Key\n✅ **Authentication**: Only authenticated approvers can access (enforced by `protocol.HTTP`)\n✅ **Purchase eligibility**: Must be submitted status, created by different user, same tenant\n✅ **Status update**: Updates from 'submitted' to 'rejected'\n✅ **All-or-nothing**: Checks all targets are eligible first; if any fail, returns 409\n✅ **RLS policies**: Uses guarded RLS via `set_config` for app.tenant, app.user, app.role\n✅ **Idempotency**: \n  - Stores fingerprint and response in ActionRequest\n  - Returns 409 if same key with different target set\n  - Returns same response with `Idempotency-Replayed=true` on replay\n  - Returns 503 if ActionRequest write fails\n✅ **Audit logging**: Records each rejection in ApprovalAudit with operation='bulk-reject.v4'\n✅ **Transaction semantics**: Status changes and audit records in single transaction\n✅ **Response format**: Returns `{\"rejectedIds\":[...]}` with IDs sorted UTF-8 order (handled by protocol.HTTP)\n✅ **Operation name**: Uses `\"bulk-reject.v4\"` constant\n✅ **Concurrency control**: Uses advisory locks by key to serialize same-key operations\n✅ **Status field**: Correctly writes only the status field to Purchase table\n\nThe implementation follows the same patterns as `bulk-approve`:\n- Same lock mechanism\n- Same eligibility checking\n- Same idempotency handling\n- Same transaction structure\n- Same error handling\n\nThe code is complete and ready for use."
